<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Apollo Grannus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> Grannus was a Gallo-Roman healing god, the Celtic deity Grannus identified with the Roman Apollo and worshipped especially at springs. His cult was strong in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a>, and his greatest sanctuary was at Grand in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vosges">Vosges</a>, where a large mosaic and an amphitheatre survive. The emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracalla">Caracalla</a> sought a cure at a shrine of Grannus in 213 CE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/apollo-grannus.jpg" alt="Apollo Grannus" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carole Raddato from FRANKFURT, Germany · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Inscription_dedicated_to_the_cult_of_Grannus_Apollo,_Roman_Grand_(Andesina),_France_(7693244124).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Grannus is the latinised form of the Gaulish Grannos, and the meaning of the name is debated. Xavier Delamarre favours a link with a Proto-Celtic word for beard, making him the bearded one, although Jürgen Zeidler notes that the god is never shown with a beard in Gallo-Roman art. Jan de Vries preferred a root meaning heat, tied to warm springs, and the name has also been compared with Old Irish grían, sun. Aquae Granni, the waters of Grannus, lies behind the modern name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a>, though that form is first recorded only in the 8th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The god is named in about thirty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> dedications, concentrated in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, the two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolt-of-the-brotherhoods">Germanies</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raetia">Raetia</a>, with outliers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dacia">Dacia</a> and as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. In roughly a fifth of them he is paired with the goddess Sirona. Many dedicants were officials and soldiers, such as imperial procurators at Inveresk, Sarmizegetusa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astorga">Astorga</a>. His best-attested cult centres are Grand in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vosges">Vosges</a> and Faimingen, ancient Phoebiana, in Raetia. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limoges">Limoges</a> the vergobret <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/postumus">Postumus</a> paid for water for the Ten Nights of Grannus, a festival that shows a genuinely Gaulish cult, at least locally.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassius-dio">Cassius Dio</a> lists Apollo Grannus with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asclepius">Asclepius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serapis">Serapis</a> among the gods to whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracalla">Caracalla</a> appealed in vain during his illness, and a Greek inscription from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a> of 217 or 218 records a citizen who travelled to the god in the emperor's entourage. Which sanctuary they sought is disputed; Andreas Hofeneder argues for Faimingen, while allowing that certainty cannot be reached.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Scholars read the god in two ways. De Vries grouped him with healing gods of springs such as Borvo, while Zeidler, noting that most sanctuaries stand at ordinary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> rather than thermal springs, saw a solar deity whose healing power lay in light and heat, alongside Sirona and Sulis. Hofeneder judges the evidence too ambiguous to establish a solar character. Grannus was usually <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a>, but at Chassenon he appears as Mars Grannus Victor and at Aquincum beside Jupiter. Bernhard Maier warns that his wide distribution belongs to the imperial period and should not be projected back into pre-Roman times.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Grannus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the conquest of Gaul in 58–51 BCE and of southern Britain in 43 CE, Celtic religion there blended with Roman religion into a syncretic Gallo-Roman faith, with gods such as Lenus Mars, <b>Apollo Grannus</b> and Sulis Minerva, while Epona was taken into Roman worship.</blockquote><a href="/religion/celtic-religion">Celtic religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Their land included sanctuaries that grew important under Rome, above all Grand, a great healing shrine of <b>Apollo Grannus</b> with a large mosaic and amphitheatre.</blockquote><a href="/state/leuci">Leuci</a></li></ul></section>
